{"author":{"id":"a66e0db0201c515c5090d5e52c036411dda9ba2f138110a926616a18a8ea0dd6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790680410,"nonce":"fFrvRxgzM3lEoSJRVCR7MN1NW8Y=","message":"Correction to comment #2:\n\nRayzor's existing `gpu` crate and `rayzor-gpu.rpkg` are the Rayzor xgpu adapter; do not add a parallel package. The migration must preserve Rayzor-owned capabilities that exceed the shared WebGPU/wgpu object surface:\n\n- `@:shader` remains a Rayzor compiler feature and continues to lower Haxe shader classes to WGSL.\n- Lazy tensor compute, fused kernel IR/codegen, quantized kernels and Metal/CUDA selection remain Rayzor extensions.\n- Generated xgpu resources and Rayzor compute extensions must share one device/queue and one typed `GpuBuffer` identity, rather than create a second wgpu context or duplicate buffer class.\n- The generated complete API and Rayzor extensions ship together in the existing `rayzor-gpu.rpkg`.\n\nxgpu owns the portable object model, IDL-derived catalog, wgpu operations and adapter-generation inputs. Rayzor owns compiler lowering, tensor semantics, fusion and backend-specific compute policy, and exposes those as extensions over xgpu handles. Packaging generates the conventional extern tree during the package build and places it in the same rpkg as the native library.","files":null}]}